<h2>Why the statutes overlap at all</h2>
<p>Each maintenance remedy was enacted for a distinct purpose. Section 144 BNSS is a speedy, secular, anti-destitution measure available irrespective of religion. Section 24 HMA funds a spouse during matrimonial litigation; Section 25 HMA settles permanent alimony when the marital tie is adjudicated. Section 18 HAMA embodies the personal-law obligation of a Hindu husband. Section 20 of the DV Act arms a Magistrate dealing with domestic violence to grant monetary relief — expenses, loss of earnings, medical costs and maintenance — and states in terms that such maintenance may be in addition to an order under the general criminal law. Parliament knew these provisions would operate on the same households; Section 36 of the DV Act accordingly declares that the Act is in addition to, and not in derogation of, other laws.</p>

<h2>The one firm rule: no double payment</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>What the law permits</h3>
    <p>Separate petitions under different statutes, before different fora, even at different times. Separate orders fixing different amounts, each valid until varied or set aside in its own proceeding.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>What the law forbids</h3>
    <p>Recovering maintenance twice over for the same period and the same need. Amounts paid under one order stand adjusted against liability under any other; in substance the payer bears the highest operative figure, not the aggregate.</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>Courts across the country have applied this adjustment principle for decades, and the Supreme Court&rsquo;s uniform guidelines on maintenance — which Delhi courts apply as a matter of course — have made disclosure and adjustment part of the standard architecture of every case: both parties file sworn affidavits of assets and income, every earlier or pending maintenance proceeding must be disclosed, and the court fixing quantum takes the existing awards into account so that orders dovetail instead of stacking.</p>

<h2>How the arithmetic works in practice</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Identify the periods.</strong> Adjustment is period-specific. A BNSS order running from the application date and an HMA interim order running from a later date overlap only where the months coincide; for non-overlapping months each order operates on its own.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Prove the payments.</strong> Only amounts actually paid are adjusted. Bank statements, receipts and court-deposit records are the currency of this exercise; cash said to have been handed over informally is routinely disbelieved.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Seek the direction.</strong> The later court should be asked, in the pleadings themselves, to fix its award taking note of the subsisting order — typically by granting the differential or by recording that payments under one order satisfy the other pro tanto.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Carry it into execution.</strong> When arrears are computed in recovery proceedings, the payer must place the cross-payments on record there too. Each forum executes its own order; the set-off travels only if it is proved in each.</div>
</div>

<h2>Drafting points for both sides</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>For claimants: disclose every prior order candidly and frame the new claim as the shortfall — concealment risks the credibility of the entire claim and rarely adds a rupee.</p>
  <p>For claimants: choose the forum for what it does best — the DV court for a composite package of residence and monetary relief, the Family Court for litigation-spanning support — rather than filing everywhere reflexively.</p>
  <p>For payers: plead the existing orders and payments specifically, annex the proof, and ask for an express adjustment direction in the order itself, not merely in argument.</p>
  <p>For payers: keep every payment traceable — bank transfer with narration, or deposit in court — so that the set-off can be established years later without a trial within a trial.</p>
  <p>For both: where quantum has genuinely changed, the remedy is an application to alter the existing order on changed circumstances, not a fresh petition elsewhere for the same relief.</p>
</div>

<h2>The practical bottom line</h2>
<p>Multiplicity of maintenance orders is a design feature of the legislation, not a defect — but it rewards the side that manages the record. A claimant who discloses fully and pitches each forum for its distinct relief faces no reduction for candour. A payer who documents payments and insists on adjustment pays once, at the highest figure a court has found justified, and no more. Most of the grief in overlapping-maintenance litigation traces to parties who did neither.</p>
<div class="note">Nothing here dilutes the independence of each order: until adjusted or varied by the court concerned, every order remains executable on its own terms. The adjustment principle is a shield against double recovery — it is not a licence to stop paying while applications are pending.</div>
